    "result_text": "Done. The full build passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\n`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/AssignCustomerCode.java` \u2014 the step that assigns `customerCode` in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline (runs before `AssignMembershipNumber`, which reads the code, so de-duplication propagates correctly).\n\nThe step now:\n1. Computes the base code as before (`<REGION>-<HASH8>`).\n2. Injects `OwnerRepository` (same injection-by-type pattern as the sibling `CheckHouseholdUnique` step) and collects existing owners' non-null `customerCode` values.\n3. If the base collides, appends `-<n>` starting at `n = 2` and increments until the result is unique, then sets that de-duplicated value.\n\n```java\nString customerCode = base;\nfor (int n = 2; existingCodes.contains(customerCode); n++) {\n    customerCode = base + \"-\" + n;\n}\nowner.setCustomerCode(customerCode);\n```\n\nI also updated the Javadoc, which previously claimed \"there are no sequence numbers,\" to describe the collision rule.\n\nThe acceptance test (`coreCustomerCodesStayUnique`) and the rest of the suite are green. Note the test asserts the rule's *guarantee* (distinct owners get distinct codes) rather than forcing a literal hash collision \u2014 as its own comment explains, telephone-uniqueness blocks an identical `normalizedTelephone + lastName` black-box, so the `-<n>` branch can't be exercised directly from an HTTP test, but the logic is in place for the genuine-collision case.",
