    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change fits this codebase's conventions cleanly:\n\n- **Right unit.** De-duplication lives in `AssignCustomerCode`, the function that already owns computing the `customerCode`. Producing a *unique* code is a natural extension of producing the code, not a separate concern that belongs elsewhere. The sibling `Assign*` functions (`AssignHousehold`, `AssignMembershipNumber`) likewise own deriving-and-setting a single owner field, so this stays consistent with the family.\n\n- **Idiomatic repository use.** Injecting `OwnerRepository` and iterating `ownerRepository.findAll()` to compare against existing owners is the established pattern here \u2014 `CheckHouseholdUnique` and `CountNamesakes` do exactly the same thing with the same `service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerRepository ownerRepository)` signature. Nothing novel or surprising for a maintainer.\n\n- **No catch-all drift.** The class retains a single public `service` method plus the private `hash8` helper; the added logic is ~12 focused lines. It isn't accreting unrelated responsibilities.\n\n- **No meaningful duplication.** The `findAll()` loop superficially resembles the ones in `CheckHouseholdUnique`/`CountNamesakes`, but each filters/accumulates differently (reject on match / count / collect codes). Those siblings already inline their own loops rather than sharing a helper, so adding a shared abstraction here would actually diverge from the codebase idiom, not improve it.\n\n- **Doc updated.** The Javadoc was revised to describe the new de-dup behavior and no longer claims \"no sequence numbers,\" keeping the contract description honest.\n\n(Scope note as instructed: I did not assess correctness \u2014 e.g. whether the just-built owner is itself included in `findAll()`, or ordering relative to `SaveOwner` \u2014 only structure.)",
